ough your cargo indeed while  the pirates in the example we just saw  were not very nice to the captain of the  greyhound they ultimately let both him  and his ship go  red though was the traditional nautical  symbol for no quarter given it was  favored by mcgrabby corsairs and used in  the caribbean as well  as a letter to the british journal about  a 1724 pirate attack explained they  hoisted jolly roger for so they call  their black ensign when they fight under  jolly roger they give quarter which they  do not when they fight under the red or  bloody flag  the purpose of the jolly roger then was  actually to remind pirate victims that  things could be worse if they didn't  cooperate  in any case such tricks were for special  occasions and pirates most frequently  sailed under national colors both  through and falls disguising themselves  as law-abiding european ships in order  to try to get close to their  unsuspecting prey or passed by any  hunters unnoticed indeed the practice of  using false flags at sea was commonly  accepted for lawful vessels as well as  pirates privateers of course were  supposed to use national colors like the  union jack  incidentally no one seems to know where  the term jolly roger comes from  there's an idea that it may be a  mispronunciation of jolie rouge the  french for pretty red  or perhaps it conflates jolie rouge and  old roger which was a colloquial english  name for the devil that dated back at  least to about seventeen hundred  the problem is that the french called  the iconic red flag a song cortier gives  no quarter not jolie rouge  also in play may be the fact that roger  has a double meaning in english it was  both a man's name and a euphemism for  the male sex organ  so the bad news is that much about the  jolly roger remains hazy  we have very few surviving examples of  pirate flags what we do have indicates  that there was quite a bit of variation  in the practice of pirate symbolism  while we have enough eyewitness ac