d you're welcome to my gold  for buy it i've lost my soul and must  die  but no one got the 90 bars in some  versions it was 200 either way just a  legend  in the end the only wealth that golden  rumor ever produced was literary  washington irving the author of rip van  winkle and the legend of sleepy hollow  wrote a set of immensely popular pirate  stories in 1824 called the money diggers  that featured dutch american farmers  tearing up their cabbage fields in  search of kids non-existent booty  and in the mid-19th century kidd became  a feature of melodrama like the highly  popular captain kid or the wizard of the  sea a romance 1839  which led credulous audiences to believe  his sunken treasure ship lays somewhere  in the hudson river  robert louis stevenson also contributed  to the myth of the buried treasure horde  his best-selling 1882 boys adventure  story treasure island and to a lesser  extent the 1889 master of ballentre  both revolve around buried treasure  plots  both helped to cement the idea of pirate  gold in the imagination of children and  their parents  the confluence of fiction and history in  stevenson is interesting  ballentre is set between 1745 and 1764  and it features a pirate who  deliberately imitates the historical  pirate blackbeard about who more shortly  stevenson doesn't date the events of  treasure island precisely but based on  the events it references it seems to  take place in the late 1750s  so that ultimate pirate novel was  composed in the late 19th century but it  stars pirates from the earlier golden  age of piracy which took place in the  caribbean and atlantic ocean from about  1650 to the end of the 1720s  stevenson also name checks several real  pirates like captain blackbeard and  captain england but he mixes in a lot of  anachronisms from the sailors he'd grown  up seeing  most of his pirates for example sport  tattoos and earrings  tattoos weren't popular among sailors  until the end of the 18th century when  south seas explorations became more  common  earrings had been fashionable for men in  the early 17th century king charles the  first reportedly wore his favorite pearl  drop earring to his own beheaded  and the sea dog walter raleigh wore  pearls in both of his ears  but with the interregnum earrings fell  out of fashion even for sailors until  the end of the 18th century as well  treasure island is a good example of how  cultures take the pirate stories of  previous eras and adjust them to fit  their own desires stevenson himself  admitted to borrowing or we might say  pirating from washington irving's money  digger stories one good pirate yarn  begets another  the interesting thing is that these  newly modified pirate fictions can then  become a part of collective folklore for  future generations  but all this is not to downplay the fact  that the historical kernels behind the  legends are often dramatic and  terrifying without the subsequent  mythologizing  and to be fair in the 17th and 18th  centuries there was no hard and fast  line between history and fiction  reportage and storytelling  the pirate edward thatch or teach alias  blackbeard stands out both as a figure  who generated extraordinary fear in his  day for his notorious deeds and as an  instance of the way pirate fact and  pirate fiction could merge to become  something entirely new  remarkable for his ferocity and defiance  the historical blackbeard does not  appear to have been an especially  wealthy pirate but he managed to  accumulate fame even without wealth his  name recognition is probably the highest  among all golden age caribbean pirates  i may have disappointed you with the  news that pirates don't talk like  pirates and that captain kidd was  neither a good pirate nor the keeper of  buried treasure so perhaps it will be of  some comfort to hear that blackbeard was  perfectly real reportedly terrifying and  he actually  did have a really memorable beard  blackbeard was a successful pirate in  terms of the damage he brought and his  legend grew partly because he destroyed  ships where many other pirates would  have just let them go after a little  plundering throwing overboard goods that  his crew had no uses for rather than  letting the unfortunate merchants retain  them  for example when boston hanged six  pirates in 1717 blackbeard vowed to burn  all new england ships he came across as  a punishment for the coastal colonies  effrontery and he is known to have  burned the ship protestant caesar and  all its cargo to the waterline  interestingly he was not particularly  murderous for a pirate even with all the  unparalleled property destruction  he seemed to get along a bit better with  the southern colonies at least for a  time though he certainly annoyed the  coastal planters  governor eden of north carolina was a  great deal less active in suppressing  piracy than the governors of virginia  and south carolina which may be related  to the inconvenient fact that some of  blackbeard's loot was found in the barn  of the governor's secretary after  blackbeard was defeated  eden hotly contested the well-published  implication that he was being bribed by  a pirate by the way but his reputation  never really recovered  whether or not he benefited from  government connivance blackbeard was  believed to be violent and was  reportedly smart about using his  reputation for violence to strike fear  into both his enemies and his own men  here's a typical anecdote of his  character from a general history of the  pirates an important work that we'll  discuss more in future lessons  one night drinking in his cabin with  hands the pilot and another man  blackbeard without any provocation  privately draws out a small pair of  pistols and [ __ ] them under the table  which being perceived by the man he  withdrew and went up on the deck leaving  hands the pilot and the captain together  when the pistols were ready he blew out  the candle and crossing his hands  discharged them at his company hands the  master was shot through the knee and  laned for life  the other pistol did no execution  being asked the meaning of this he only  answered by damning them that if he did  not now and then kill one of them they  would forget who he was  as is true of much in the general  history  it's hard to know exactly how far to  trust this story it speaks of thatch's  reputation but is not necessarily  consistent with the facts  israel hands had apparently been a  trusted boson to blackbeard often  commanding ships that sailed in consort  with thatch  hands was not among the pirates captured  when blackbeard was ultimately defeated  but was instead arrested in bath north  carolina where he may have been  recovering from a knee wound or maybe  wasn't  he received a pardon for dustifying  against the other pirates and was one of  only two in the crew to escape being  hanged  hansa's name would reappear centuries  later in stevenson's treasure island  where he was a major antagonist to the  main character jim hawkins although in  treasure island hans was said to have  sailed with captain flint and he is shot  to death by jim not in the knee by a  drunken pirate  but back to blackbeard's black beard  which according to lieutenant maynard  was so long and glorious that he tied it  up in black ribbons  you should know that in the 1720s  western european men wore their faces  clean-shaven  even infamous and feared pirates like  captain bartholomew roberts and other  pirates are consistently depicted this  way in illustrations sometimes they're  even wearing wigs too some men shave  their heads as well as their faces to  the english wearing a beard at all  marked a man as alien and maybe a little  bit barbaric  there may have been some connection  between the horror attached to  blackbeard's visage and the  mediterranean corsairs who were far more  likely than european pirates to wear  beards  the name of the famous corsairing  barbarossa brothers whom we'll