re  before battle and he's often depicted  that way in later illustrations most  recently take a look at the posters for  pirates of the caribbean on stranger  tides that feature actor ian mcshane's  blackbeard with glowing beard tendrils  but that's because contemporary  illustrations which were often pretty  rough made it easy to confuse his beard  which he tucked over his ears with the  sparklers hanging down from his hat  edward thatcher's death was as extreme  as his life and predictably generated  accounts with varying degrees of  adherence to reality  in fact the american author and  statesman benjamin franklin claimed that  one of his first publications done at  the tender age of 13 was a popular  ballot about blackbeard's death entitled  the downfall of piracy  it was really pretty bad  will you hear of a bloody battle lately  fought upon the seas it will make your  ears to rattle and your admiration seize  in contrast to bows that depicted  pirates as bold rebels like those  centered on the englishman john ward or  henry every franklin's aim was to  glorify not the pirate but lieutenant  maynard and the sailors of the royal  navy who defeated blackbeard and the  crew of his ship the adventure in 1718  near ocracoke island north carolina  this would have surely pleased the  governor of virginia who thought  ocracoke was being turned into a  pirate's stronghold  neighboring colonial governors often had  divergent views of piracy  but whatever its actual details the  battle does indeed appear to have been  bloody and dramatic as maynard's men  strained aboard thatch's ship at least  according to maynard's self-serving  account later published in the weekly  journal of british gazetteer  the pirate raised a glass and drank  damnation to me and my men whom he  styled cowardly puppies saying he would  neither give nor take quarter  the boston newsletter supplies the  detail that after blackbeard had wounded  maynard's sword hand and apparently  survived a pistol shot he was brought  down by a powerful scottish highlander  who gave teach a cut on the neck teach  saying well done lad the highlander  replied if it be not well done i'll do  it better with that he gave him a second  stroke which cut off his head laying it  flat on his shoulder  this detail has the air of fiction about  it and the general history in contrast  suggests that blackbeard simply  succumbed suddenly to his wounds  but it's certain blackbeard fell in  battle  maynard recorded that thatch's corpse  showed at least five bullet holes and  some 20 sword cuts all over his body and  though we're not sure who beheaded him  we do know that the lieutenant hung his  disembodied head from the ship's prow  both the general history and the  governor of virginia recorded that  blackbeard had left a man below with  orders to blow up his ship in the event  of defeat and that the plot was narrowly  averted by the intervention of a  prisoner samuel o'dell who was arrested  with the pirates but later acquitted at  trial  of course the general history also  relates accounts from surviving members  of blackbeard's crew that the devil  himself was among them on that fateful  night so you can see the challenge that  it presents to historians  as i mentioned earlier  blackbeard's piracy doesn't appear to  have made him rich  maynard's victorious men eagerly  searched the adventure for booty but  found only some gold dust and a few  small bits of stolen silver here and  there along with stolen cocoa and some  sugar  lest we be overly persuaded by  franklin's heroic vision of maynard it's  worth noting that he enslaved and sold  the african men he found among  blackbeard's surviving crew to increase  his profit threshold  the wreck of what may very well be  blackbeard's other famous ship the queen  anne's revenge was located and  excavation began in 1996  objects found aboard are as varied as  belt buckles to wine bottles to an enema  syringe but there's not much sign of  treasure  nevertheless blackbeard's legend rapidly  morphed to incorporate any number of  outlandish claims  in 1724 for example charles johnson  depicted him as a despoiler of virgins  and a polygamist although there are no  other contemporary claims about his  sexual proclivities being anything  remarkable  and as his name recognition grew it  became curiously detached from the man  himself  and instead turned into something of a  free-floating signifier for anything  pirate-like at all  you can get a sense of this in one of  the most popular melodramas of the  entire 19th century one that we'll  return to later on in the series john  cartwright crosses tragic comedy  blackbeard or the captive princess  it's the musical and special effects  spectacular that features blackbeard  hoarding a massive treasure in  madagascar capturing and falling in love  with the princess of the mughal empire  and being haunted by the ghost of the  jealous first wife he'd murdered  this very different depiction of  blackbeard is still bold and commanding  and interestingly he's still brought  down by a heroic lieutenant maynard in  the fiery last act but there ends the  resemblance  the effect of this hugely popular  reimagining of blackbeard away from the  carolina coastline and into the bosom of  a fainting female captive gave great  power to the long tradition of the  lustful stage swashbuckler  yet in large part the lovelorn pirate  was an invention of 19th century popular  culture given legitimacy by its  attachment to works by sir walter scott  and the brooding george gordon lord  byron  what can we take from this admittedly  confusing mix of pirate reality and  imagination  i'd emphasize that the uncertainties and  ambiguities that surround real pirates  of the golden age were there from the  very start  and even the most outlandish stories we  tell ourselves and our kids today often  have at least a small archival basis  walking the plank though was not  something pirates routinely