counts  of the jolly roger to have some idea of  what such a flag could look like the  origin of the standard remains murky  we don't quite know why it became  popular among pirates or why they called  it that  but the good news is that there's plenty  of basis to believe in this particular  pirate legend  alas we cannot say the same for probably  the second most beloved pirate legend  the trove of buried treasure  as we'll explore in greater detail in a  future discussion this just wasn't very  likely to happen  most of your standard golden age pirates  did the maritime equivalent of robbing a  couple of 7-elevens sold what they could  and then drifted away into a quiet  mostly law abiding retirement the  average length of a pirate career was no  more than one to three years  a few pirates did strike real wealth  some of the men who followed thomas ii  henry every and william kidd walked away  with hundreds of pounds even by  conservative estimates  but this was not the common case  and such lucky pirates had no  predilection for burying their hordes  like maritime dragons  what money they acquired they tended to  spend quickly the medieval and viking  hordes that we still find from time to  time are usually theorized to be either  burial treasures or treasure buried to  protect it from pirates rather than done  by pirates  captain kidd whom you'll recall from the  previous lesson was not much of a pirate  was rumored to have buried a cache of  spoils on gardner's island in new york  but this seems unlikely it's true that  fearing arrest kid had tried to disperse  and hide much of his booty but he did so  in the more conventional way of buying  and selling and leaving it with various  people most of whom were found and  persuaded to cough up the ill-gotten  gains by his persecutor the earl of  belmont  one version of the anonymous 18th  century ballad of captain kid promises i  90 bars of gold and dollars manifold  with riches uncontrolled come all ye  young and old you're welcome to my gold  for buy it i've lost my soul and must  die  but no one got the 90 bars in some  versions it was 200 either way just a  legend  in the end the only wealth that golden  rumor ever produced was literary  washington irving the author of rip van  winkle and the legend of sleepy hollow  wrote a set of immensely popular pirate  stories in 1824 called the money diggers  that featured dutch american farmers  tearing up their cabbage fields in  search of kids non-existent booty  and in the mid-19th century kidd became  a feature of melodrama like the highly  popular captain kid or the wizard of the  sea a romance 1839  which led credulous audiences to believe  his sunken treasure ship lays somewhere  in the hudson river  robert louis stevenson also contributed  to the myth of the buried treasure horde  his best-selling 1882 boys adventure  story treasure island and to a lesser  extent the 1889 master of ballentre  both revolve around buried treasure  plots  both helped to cement the idea of pirate  gold in the imagination of children and  their parents  the confluence of fiction and history in  stevenson is interesting  ballentre is set between 1745 and 1764  and it features a pirate who  deliberately imitates the historical  pirate blackbeard about who more shortly  stevenson doesn't date the events of  treasure island precisely but based on  the events it references it seems to  take place in the late 1750s  so that ultimate pirate novel was  composed in the late 19th century but it  stars pirates from the earlier golden  age of piracy which took place in the  caribbean and atlantic ocean from about  1650 to the end of the 1720s  stevenson also name checks several real  pirates like captain blackbeard and  captain england but he mixes in a lot of  anachronisms from the sailors he'd grown  up seeing  most of his pirates for example sport  tattoos and earrings  tattoos weren't popular among sailors  until the end of the 18th century when  south seas explorations became more  common  earrings had been fashionable for men in  the early 17th century king charles the  first reportedly wore his favorite pearl  drop earring to his own beheaded  and the sea dog walter raleigh wore  pearls in both of his ears  but with the interregnum earrings fell  out of fashion even for sailors until  the end of the 18th century as well  treasure island is a good example of how  cultures take the pirate stories of  previous eras and adjust them to fit  their own desires stevenson himself  admitted to borrowing or we might say  pirating from washington irving's money  digger stories one good pirate yarn  begets another  the interesting thing is that these  newly modified pirate fictions can then  become a part of collective folklore for  future generations  but all this is not to downplay the fact  that the historical kernels behind the  legends are often dramatic and  terrifying without the subsequent  mythologizing  and to be fair in the 17th and 18th  centuries there was no hard and fast  line between history and fiction  reportage and storytelling  the pirate edward thatch or teach alias  blackbeard stands out both as a figure  who generated extraordinary fear in his  day for his notorious deeds and as an  instance of the way pirate fact and  pirate fiction could merge to become  something entirely new  remarkable for his ferocity and defiance  the historical blackbeard does not  appear to have been an especially  wealthy pirate but he managed to  accumulate fame even without wealth his  name recognition is probably the highest  among all golden age caribbean pirates  i may have disappointed you with the  news that pirates don't talk like  pirates and that captain kidd was  neither a good pirate nor the keeper of  buried treasure so perhaps it will be of  some comfort to hear that blackbeard was  perfectly real reportedly terrifying and  he actually  did have a really memorable beard  blackbeard was a successful pirate in  terms of the damage he brought and his  legend grew partly because he destroyed  ships where many other pirates would  have just let them go after a little  plundering throwing overboard goods that  his crew had no uses for rather than  letting the unfortunate merchants retain  them  for example when boston hanged six  pirates in 1717 blackbeard vowed to burn  all new england ships he came across as  a punishment for the coastal colonies  effrontery and he is known to have  burned the ship protestant caesar and  all its cargo to the waterline  interestingly he was not particularly  murderous for a pirate even with all the  unparalleled property destruction  he seemed to get along a bit better with  the southern colonies at least for a  time though he certainly annoyed the  coastal planters  governor eden of north carolina was a  great deal less active in suppressing  piracy than the governors of virginia  and south carolina which may be related  to the inconvenient fact that some of  blackbeard's loot was found in the barn  of the governor's secretary after  blackbeard was defeated  eden hotly contested the well-published  implication that he was being bribed by  a pirate by the way but his reputation  never really recovered  whether or not he benefited from  government connivance blackbeard was  believed to be violent and was  reportedly smart about using his  reputation for violence to strike fear  into both his enemies and his own men  here's a typical anecdote of his  character from a general history of the  pirates an important work that we'll  discuss more in future lessons  one night drinking in his cabin with  hands the pilot and another man  blackbeard without any provocation  privately draws out a small pair of  pistols and [ __ ] them under the table  which being perceived by the man he  withdrew and went up on the deck leaving  hands the pilot and the captain together  when the pistols were ready he blew out  the candle and crossing his hands  discharged them at his company hands the  master was shot through the knee and  laned for life  the other pistol did no execution  being asked the meaning of this he only  answered by damning them that if he did  not now and then kill one of them they  would forget who he was  as is true of much in the general  history  it's hard to know exactly how far to  trust this story it speaks of thatch's  reputation but is not necessarily  consistent with the facts  israel hands had apparently been a  trusted boson to blackbeard often  commanding ships that sailed in consort  with thatch  hands was not among the pirates captured  when blackbeard was ultimately defeated  but was instead arrested in bath north  carolina where he may have been  recovering from a knee wound or maybe  wasn't  he received a pardon for dustifying  against the other pirates and was one of  only two in the crew to escape being  hanged  hansa's name would reappear centuries  later in stevenson's treasure island  where he was a major antagonist to the  main character jim hawkins although in  treasure island hans was said to have  sailed with captain flint and he is shot  to death by jim not in the knee by a  drunken pirate  but back to blackbeard's black beard  which according to lieutenant maynard  was so long and glorious that he tied it  up in black ribbons  you should know that in the 1720s  western european men wore their faces  clean-shaven  even infamous and feared pirates like  captain bartholomew roberts and other  pirates are consistently depicted this  way in illustrations sometimes they're  even wearing wigs too some men shave  their heads as well as their faces to  the english wearing a beard at all  marked a man as alien and maybe a little  bit barbaric  there may have been some connection  between the horror attached to  blackbeard's visage and the  mediterranean corsairs who were far more  likely than european pirates to wear  beards  the name of the famous corsairing  barbarossa brothers whom we'll return to  another day means red beard in italian  although portraits show them as being  dark-haired  thatch's facial hair in connecting him  to barbary corsairs could have seemed  like a rejection of european identity  and norms  and of christianity as a part of that  thatch's kind of brutal rebellion  against european norms would have been  in character for a man who named one of  his flagships the queen anne's revenge  that name suggested a political  allegiance to the jacobite movement  which at its extreme end advocated the  overthrow of england's then-reigning  royal family the hanoverians and the  restoration of the stewart dynasty  blackbeard's jacobitism seems to have  been a tone or a mood rather than a  matter of actual allegiance though there  was no evidence that he had any active  connection to trying to restore the  stewards  the point it seems was to enhance his  fearsome reputation and he apparently  went to some pains to do so  the most vivid description of his  appearance as usual comes from the  general history of the pirates  captain teach we are told  assumed the cognamen of blackbeard from  that large quantity of hair which like a  frightful meteor covered his whole face  and frightened america more than any  comet that has appeared there a long  time  this beard was black which he suffered  to grow of an extravagant length as to  bread that came up to his eyes he was  accustomed to twist it with ribbons in  small tales and turn them about his ears  in time of action he stuck lighted  matches under his hat which appearing on  each side of his face his eyes naturally  looking fierce and wild made him  altogether such a figure that  imagination cannot form an idea of a  fury from hell to look more frightful  this image understandably captured the  imagination of the public and circulated  in wood cuts and engravings as well as  words  and as it circulated it was embellished  for example the notion arose that  blackbeard let his own beard on fire  before battle and he's often depicted  that way in later illustrations most  recently take a look at the posters for  pirates of the caribbean on stranger  tides that feature actor ian mcshane's  blackbeard with glowing beard tendrils  but that's because contemporary  illustrations which were often pretty  rough made it easy to confuse his beard  which he tucked over his ears with the  sparklers hanging down from his hat  edward thatcher's death was as extreme  as his life and predictably generated  accounts with varying degrees of  adherence to reality  in fact the american author and  statesman benjamin franklin claimed that  one of his first publications done at  the tender age of 13 was a popular  ballot about blackbeard's death entitled  the downfall of piracy  it was really pretty bad  will you hear of a bloody battle lately  fought upon the seas it will make your  ears to rattle and your admiration seize  in contrast to bows that depicted  pirates as bold rebels like those  centered on the englishman john ward or  henry every franklin's aim was to  glorify not the pirate but lieutenant  maynard and the sailors of the royal  navy who defeated blackbeard and the  crew of his ship the adventure in 1718  near ocracoke island north carolina  this would have surely pleased the  governor of virginia who thought  ocracoke was being turned into a  pirate's stronghold  neighboring colonial governors often had  divergent views of piracy  but whatever its actual details the  battle does indeed appear to have been  bloody and dramatic as maynard's men  strained aboard thatch's ship at least  according to maynard's self-serving  account later published in the weekly  journal of british gazetteer  the pirate raised a glass and drank  damnation to me and my men whom he  styled cowardly puppies saying he would  neither give nor take quarter  the boston newsletter supplies the  detail that after blackbeard had wounded  maynard's sword hand and apparently  survived a pistol shot he was brought  down by a powerful scottish highlander  who gave teach a cut on the neck teach  saying well done lad the highlander  replied if it be not well done i'll do  it better with that he gave him a second  stroke which cut off his head laying it  flat on his shoulder  this detail has the air of fiction about  it and the general history in contrast  suggests that blackbeard simply  succumbed suddenly to his wounds  but it's certain blackbeard fell in  battle  maynard recorded that thatch's corpse  showed at least five bullet holes and  some 20 sword cuts all over his body and  though we're not sure who beheaded him  we do know that the lieutenant hung his  disembodied head from the ship's prow  both the general history and the  governor of virginia recorded that  blackbeard had left a man below with  orders to blow up his ship in the event  of defeat and that the plot was narrowly  averted by the intervention of a  prisoner samuel o'dell who was arrested  with the pirates but later acquitted at  trial  of course the general history also  relates accounts from surviving members  of blackbeard's crew that the devil  himself was among them on that fateful  night so you can see the challenge that  it presents to historians  as i mentioned earlier  blackbeard's piracy doesn't appear to  have made him rich  maynard's victorious men eagerly  searched the adventure for booty but  found only some gold dust and a few  small bits of stolen silver here and  there along with stolen cocoa and some  sugar  lest we be overly persuaded by  franklin's heroic vision of maynard it's  worth noting that he enslaved and sold  the african men he found among  blackbeard's surviving crew to increase  his profit threshold  the wreck of what may very well be  blackbeard's other famous ship the queen  anne's revenge was located and  excavation began in 1996  objects found aboard are as varied as  belt buckles to wine bottles to an enema  syringe but there's not much sign of  treasure  nevertheless blackbeard's legend rapidly  morphed to incorporate any number of  outlandish claims  in 1724 for example charles johnson  depicted him as a despoiler of virgins  and a polygamist although there are no  other contemporary claims about his  sexual proclivities being anything  remarkable  and as his name recognition grew it  became curiously detached from the man  himself  and instead turned into something of a  free-floating signifier for anything  pirate-like at all  you can get a sense of this in one of  the most popular melodramas of the  entire 19th century one that we'll  return to later on in the series john  cartwright crosses tragic comedy  blackbeard or the captive princess  it's the musical and special effects  spectacular that features blackbeard  hoarding a massive treasure in  madagascar capturing and falling in love  with the princess of the mughal empire  and being haunted by the ghost of the  jealous first wife he'd murdered  this very different depiction of  blackbeard is still bold and commanding  and interestingly he's still brought  down by a heroic lieutenant maynard in  the fiery last act but there ends the  resemblance  the effect of this hugely popular  reimagining of blackbeard away from the  carolina coastline and into the bosom of  a fainting female captive gave great  power to the long tradition of the  lustful stage swashbuckler  yet in large part the lovelorn pirate  was an invention of 19th century popular  culture given legitimacy by its  attachment to works by sir walter scott  and the brooding george gordon lord  byron  what can we take from this admittedly  confusing mix of pirate reality and  imagination  i'd emphasize that the uncertainties and  ambiguities that surround real pirates  of the golden age were there from the  very start  and even the most outlandish stories we  tell ourselves and our kids today often  have at least a small archival basis  walking the plank though was not  something pirates routinely made people  do  stay tuned to find out why
 

